---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const phaseCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '0.35*(x - 0.01)', label: 'solid–liquid boundary (melting)', color: '#1d4ed8' },
    { expr: '0.02 + 0.12*log(x/0.006)', label: 'liquid–gas boundary (boiling)', color: '#d9480f', width: 2.2 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0.006, max: 0.04, label: 'pressure (schematic)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 1, label: 'temperature (schematic)' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 340,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/states-of-matter">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Solid, liquid, gas — three answers to one question: <strong>how tightly do molecules cling to
    each other?</strong> Strong cling with fixed positions = solid; strong cling with mobile
    positions = liquid; barely clinging = gas. Adding energy weakens the grip, so matter moves
    between states — and a pressure–temperature map (a <em>phase diagram</em>) shows exactly
    which state wins where.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a schematic phase map — the two boundaries meet at the triple point where all three states coexist" widget="plotter" config={phaseCfg} />

  <p>
    The crossing of the two boundaries is the <strong>triple point</strong> — for water, at
    0.01 °C and 611 Pa, all three phases share a flask. Below that pressure, liquid water cannot
    exist at all: ice sublimates straight to vapour (which is why Martian water skips the liquid
    phase, and why your frost-free freezer shrinks ice cubes).
  </p>

  <h2>State changes and their bookkeeping</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Energy goes in even without temperature rising.</strong> Melting ice stays at 0 °C while it absorbs 334 J/g — the <em>latent heat</em> unglues the lattice. Sweating works because evaporating water carries away ~2,260 J/g.</li>
    <li><strong>Boiling is a pressure phenomenon.</strong> Water boils when its vapour pressure matches the surroundings — hence 100 °C at sea level but ~71 °C on Everest, and pressure cookers push it to ~121 °C.</li>
    <li><strong>Gases fill any container</strong> because their molecules fly ~500 m/s with almost no mutual grip; solids hold shape because their molecules vibrate in place around fixed lattice sites.</li>
    <li><strong>Plasma</strong> is the fourth state: so hot that electrons detach. It is the most common state in the universe (stars) and the rarest in daily life (flames, lightning, neon signs).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Liquids and Solids); Atkins, Physical Chemistry.</p>
</TopicPage>
